  "summary_text": "Kyle, Friday delivered solid momentum at Port Washington but exposed a structural problem at Hicksville that needs immediate attention.\n\nPort Washington is running hot. Yesterday's $3,431 (up 22% YoY) came from four groomer-days at $527 per groomer\u2014well above the 30-day baseline of $468. Retail jumped 42%, add-on attachment climbed to 68%, and bather hours spiked to 7.9h. Week-to-date is up 21%. Month-to-date tells the real story: you're down 5% in absolute dollars, but down 7.7% against the DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014meaning this is real performance loss, not calendar mix. The gap signals you're carrying fewer groomer-days than September (100 vs 105 MTD), and productivity per groomer is down $30 day ($471 vs $501). You should be proud of the team's execution yesterday, but the month-end trend suggests capacity constraints are hitting harder than last month.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Yesterday dropped 19% on only five groomers at $396 each\u2014below baseline. More troubling: add-on attachment collapsed to 33% (down 5 points) while core grooms fell 18% week-over-week. MTD you're ahead +17% on appointments, yet you're scheduling only one groomer today and zero tomorrow. Glen Cove has nothing on the calendar for the next seven days. Month-to-date Hicksville is outpacing September in appointment growth, but the staffing cliff ahead will kill that momentum. You have a reactivation pool worth $457k across both stores; you cannot execute against it without bather and groomer coverage.\n\n**Coverage:** Today Port Washington runs 4 groomer-days with 8 bather hours; Hicksville 4 groomer-days with zero bathers; Glen Cove dark. Tomorrow Port Washington drops to 1 groomer with 15 bather hours. Next seven days: 58 groomer-days total (36 Port, 22 Hicksville, 0 Glen Cove) and 83 bather hours."
